WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Scheduling is one of the most practical ways to reduce GPU waste.

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:14.400
When teams share a limited pool through queues or job schedulers, they can prioritize important work, avoid over-allocation, and make idle capacity visible.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.600
A queue also creates a natural place to apply limits and ownership rules.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:25.600
Idle shutdown policies are especially valuable for notebooks, development workers, test clusters, and temporary endpoints.

5
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:33.600
If a resource has no useful work, no active owner, or no current schedule, it should not quietly consume budget.

6
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.400
Shutdown should be predictable and communicated so teams can save work properly.

7
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.600
Interruptible capacity can be useful when a job can tolerate stops and restarts.

8
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.000
That requires checkpointing, retry limits, idempotent job behavior, and clear expectations.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:56.000
It is not a universal answer, but it can be a strong fit for some batch, training, and evaluation work.

10
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.200
Timeouts, priorities, and retry limits prevent runaway execution.

11
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.000
A failed job should not restart forever.

12
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000
A low-priority experiment should not block urgent production work.

13
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.400
A scheduled window can align expensive processing with real business need.

14
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.800
Cleanup automation closes the loop.

15
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:19.600
Volumes, temporary datasets, checkpoints, unused endpoints, stale images, and generated artifacts should have owners and retention rules.

16
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.200
Reducing idle time often saves money without changing the model or reducing useful output.

17
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.600
Scheduling also protects people.

18
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:36.000
When queues, priorities, and windows are clear, teams spend less time negotiating access informally and more time improving the work itself.

19
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.400
Predictable scheduling makes cost control feel like a normal operating practice.
